WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:08.150
Hello, everyone.

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:08.650
Welcome.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:09.940
My name is Sanjay Sarma.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.850
I'm Professor of Mechanical
Engineering at MIT,

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:15.580
and I'm also the Vice
President for Open Learning.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.470
What a wonderful
gathering this is.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.210
This is really
siblings and cousins

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:24.220
and brothers and sisters in
the open education movement.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:29.020
Knowledge is like oxygen. I
hope no one figures out how

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.600
to charge for oxygen.
I hope they figure out

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.057
a way in which we never
charge for knowledge.

00:00:34.057 --> 00:00:35.140
It should be like the air.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.330
We should breathe it.

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:39.880
We are at a very
interesting point in time.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.100
MIT faculty speaking
for my own colleagues

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.650
have an opportunity to
learn from senior leaders

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:47.750
at HBCUs, who are
promoting education

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:52.270
equity by spearheading OER,
open education resources

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.790
within their institutions.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:58.720
And we are looking at MIT to
include culturally relevant

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.610
materials in our own courses.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:04.690
This effort is an invaluable
resource for discovering more

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.660
a more inclusive canon of OER.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.790
The hope really is that
my colleagues adapt

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:14.200
these OER materials created
by HBCUs and colleagues

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.980
in our own courses here,
and then we share them back

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:21.550
with the world through
OpenCourseWare.

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:23.830
And so this way,
we can collectively

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:28.030
promote educational equity, not
only within our MIT community,

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:31.540
but in the nation,
and perhaps the world.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:35.570
The question really for us today
is, how can we do this today?

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:36.980
How can we do this together?

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.560
And this is just the
first conversation.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:44.900
How do we establish a metabolism
of open educational resources,

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:50.350
especially around
equity and around,

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:53.110
in this particular case,
for our discussion,

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:57.500
around materials that
are culturally relevant.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.520
So I would like to
particularly thank

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.770
two colleagues, Robbie
Melton and Gerry

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:04.390
Hanley for doing
this great event,

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.760
and the amazing panelists.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.479
And I welcome you to this.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:11.020
And with that, I'll hand off
to my friend and colleague,

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:14.140
Curt Newton from MIT.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:14.890
Thank you, Sanjay.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:19.240
Just a couple of housekeeping
notes before we dive in.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:23.410
We're going to try to use
the chat collectively,

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:25.120
in the spirit of conversation.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.730
As we're going, please enter
your questions in the chat,

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:31.030
and I'll encourage
our presenters here

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:35.980
to engage, and feel free to
answer directly as we're going.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.240
We are recording.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:42.182
And as we get into
the Q&amp;A at the end,

00:02:42.182 --> 00:02:43.890
if you don't want to
appear in the video,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:47.270
I suggest that you keep
your video turned off.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:53.020
I'd like to thank, along with
all of our participants here

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:57.280
today who have joined us, also
thank the Hewlett Foundation

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.690
for facilitating this
introduction between MIT Open

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:05.260
Learning and these HBCU
leaders, and to our colleagues

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:09.040
across the Open 2030 working
group for the inspiration

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:10.480
over the past couple of years.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.380
And again, just reiterating
Sanjay's closing remark,

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:17.000
we hope that this is the
first of what will be many,

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.510
many ongoing conversations.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:26.680
And with that, I'd like to
hand over to Dr. Robbie Melton.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:28.130
Hello, everyone.

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:29.650
I'm Robbie Melton.

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:38.020
And today, we are going to
help history in the making.

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.060
Again, I'm Robbie Melton.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:43.600
On behalf of the
Historically Black Colleges

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:47.830
and University and
Tennessee State University,

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:52.630
we are here to share our
passion, our purpose.

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:59.620
But today, we are all,
colleagues, will make history.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:03.520
I already had a
presentation for you,

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.860
and you will see by my slides.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:10.630
But I'm going to go
off script on purpose.

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:16.329
Ladies and gentlemen, on behalf
of all educators in the world,

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:20.709
we all are in a war.

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:25.180
Today we're not going to just
talk about open education.

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:28.600
We're going to do
something about it

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.650
on behalf of the
people of Ukraine,

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:37.900
who are now leaving their
homes, leaving their schools,

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:41.680
leaving their history.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.330
Today, we're going to
share with you why we

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:48.880
have the cultural collection.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:52.390
We are here to
demonstrate, all of us,

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:58.690
what we can do with the
mighty tool of technology.

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:02.830
Today as we present
and share with you

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:06.250
the purpose of a
cultural collection,

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:12.310
not only for the HBCU,
but for the world,

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:21.310
we dedicate this presentation to
those diversity inclusive, all

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:23.410
of those words that we hear.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:25.940
Today we're going to
do something about it.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:30.550
So I have my team
already in the chat.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:34.220
And as we go through
this presentation,

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:37.060
this is what we're going to do.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:43.330
We are going to capture
the culture of the Ukraine,

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:49.000
as they try to take it away,
with open education, which

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.190
means open to everyone.

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:56.470
I'm going to have you, because
you know how to multitask,

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:59.110
while you're at
your computer, I'm

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:06.880
going to have you to find
on the internet history,

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:11.350
famous people, whatever
about the Ukraine,

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:16.060
and you're going to put
that URL in the chat.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:22.660
My team will capture
that URL highlighting

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:25.300
the Ukraine and
their contribution

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:32.020
to education, because by the
time you wake up tomorrow

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:37.360
morning, we will have a
cultural collection on behalf

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:39.220
of the Ukrainian people.

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:45.100
They will not lose their
history, nor their education.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:49.510
All of you should celebrate
on behalf of the Hewlett

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:53.740
Foundation why a
cultural collection

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:57.700
is so vital to open education.

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:02.620
And with that, we are
all now responsible,

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:05.920
because we have the
tool of technology

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:11.780
to help underserved population,
and we're going to do it today.

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:14.410
So with that,
everyone, I'm going

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:18.040
to go through my presentation,
but you have homework to do.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:22.180
While you're at that
computer listening,

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:24.670
I need for you to
start searching.

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:26.200
Get on Google.

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:30.730
Get on those search engine,
and find information

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:34.630
about the Ukraine,
so that we can put it

00:07:34.630 --> 00:07:37.030
in a special collection.

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:40.730
That's what we can do together.

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:44.630
So with that, I will
start my presentation.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:49.720
But we are here
again for a purpose.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:52.590
The purpose of the
cultural collection

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:59.670
for the HBCU, the same as every
other underserved population.

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:06.480
It is to bring to the world our
contribution, our achievement,

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:09.960
and to share, share, share.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:16.860
So with that, on behalf
of the 106 HBCU sisters,

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:23.430
we come to you with a
curation of our history,

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:28.390
our achievement, how about our
distinguished faculty members.

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:33.940
So as we roll this
away, we again,

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:37.840
can change this whole world.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:40.090
And we can do
something about it.

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:42.960
So with that, again,
the next slide

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.490
we'll share with you,
our cultural collection,

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:52.680
and our underlying
purpose right now.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:09:00.120
Everybody, students cannot learn
from books they can't afford.

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.670
We all know that.

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:06.600
And unfortunately,
in our day, people

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:09.000
are trying to restrict books.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.190
They're trying to destroy books.

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:20.010
We together, all 75 of us,
can make sure we have access

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:22.650
to education and books.

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:24.520
Next slide, please.

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:29.550
So with that, everyone, you
all know the cost of textbooks,

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:32.250
here are some old
numbers, because inflation

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:35.550
already jumped up the
price of textbooks.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:39.120
Right now, the
average cost $1,200.

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:42.240
You might think, wow, that's OK.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:43.950
No, it's not OK.

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:48.870
Again, students cannot learn
from books they cannot afford.

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:50.410
Next slide.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:55.230
So with that, let me just share
with you the impact of textbook

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:57.750
costs on students.

00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:59.550
Again, you see the statistics.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:01.890
Some take fewer courses.

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:04.980
Some don't even register
for a specific course,

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:09.470
or how about you just
drop out or withdraw.

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:13.220
Here's the one that
you should center on.

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:16.460
Earned a poor grade
because I could not

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:20.030
afford to buy the textbook.

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:22.310
Fail a course because
I couldn't afford.

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:24.440
Or guess what, everyone?

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:28.310
You don't purchase the
required book at all.

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:29.660
Open education.

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:31.520
That's what we are here for.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:37.100
But within open education,
next slide, please,

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:40.180
we missed a little component.

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:44.340
And so we were able
to conduct, hot off

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:48.690
the press, a faculty and
students survey report.

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:51.390
And OK, you all know.

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:54.060
We know that we
need to inform more.

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:56.340
We need to contribute more.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:58.900
But guess what else we found?

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:04.500
We found, next slide, that we
were missing a whole component

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:07.650
of cultural selection.

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:11.400
Our students, our faculty,
our communities kept saying,

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:15.760
thank you for open education,
thank you for a free textbook,

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:18.610
thank you for affordable
learning solution.

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:22.200
However, next slide,
please, you will

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:27.600
see that they also found
that they didn't find

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.300
culturally relevant materials.

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:31.710
Look at the numbers.

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:34.170
This should say something.

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:41.850
Again, we have in our hands
the tools, the knowledge

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:43.710
to do something about this.

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:47.280
So on behalf of the
HBCU, guess what?

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:51.780
We formed through a partnership
with MERLOT, MERLOT.org--

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:54.630
all right, everybody put
MERLOT.org right there.

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:58.800
Because Gerry Hanley, who you're
going to hear in a few minutes,

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:04.890
stepped up with his open
education portal, to say let's

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:06.220
do something about it.

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:08.380
So that's why you are here.

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:11.760
We're here to share with you
not what we're going to do,

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:13.590
quote unquote.

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:16.650
We're here to share with
you what we have completed.

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:19.500
The next slide
we'll share with you

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:24.360
on behalf of the support from
the William and Flora Hewlett

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:25.350
Foundation.

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:32.670
And yes, I'm going to call out
Angela, yes, Angela B. Barger.

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:40.620
She-- listen, the Hewlett
Foundation said there's a need.

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:43.020
And ladies and
gentlemen, I'm here

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:47.400
to present to you with a
panel, a distinguished panel,

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:51.330
and now with the
partnership of MIT,

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:57.780
that we are able to curate,
highlight, showcase, and give

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:04.140
you just a little taste of
our HBCU cultural collection.

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:04.900
Next slide.

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:06.340
Thank you.

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:10.020
So on behalf, again, of
the historically Black

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:15.450
college and University, you must
be asking, again, the purpose.

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:19.450
The purpose, you're going
to learn something today.

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:22.290
Number two, you're going
to do something today.

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:25.620
Number three, on
behalf of the HBCU,

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:30.900
as we celebrate all cultures.

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:32.380
Next slide.

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:36.270
So I am pleased to share
with you, I need a drum roll,

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:40.140
that our cultural
collection includes

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:43.950
not just general information,
biography, Black History Month,

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:51.100
HBCU history, but we are able
to address diversity, equity,

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:57.930
inclusiveness, all within
this open education portal.

00:13:57.930 --> 00:14:04.290
Again, access from all over the
world that any time, any place,

00:14:04.290 --> 00:14:09.330
any type of whatever that
can hook up to that internet,

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:16.140
you have this rich resource
right there at your

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:16.980
[INAUDIBLE].

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:20.410
So with that, with our cultural
collection-- next slide,

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:21.750
please--

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:25.710
I'm here to highlight
again our need.

00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:26.520
And guess what?

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:28.710
They're going to put it
in the chat, right now.

00:14:28.710 --> 00:14:31.560
Because you can go
to it right now.

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:33.840
And again, just think.

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:38.040
You all logged on only
