materials from a recognition
that we need everyone

00:48:24.590 --> 00:48:28.430
everywhere working together
on global challenges,

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:31.610
and we need knowledge
and other tools to do so.

00:48:31.610 --> 00:48:38.570
Now, many people come to MIT
for STEM resources, right?

00:48:38.570 --> 00:48:41.090
We think we've reached
over half a billion people

00:48:41.090 --> 00:48:41.810
around the world.

00:48:41.810 --> 00:48:44.450
Students, teachers,
independent learners,

00:48:44.450 --> 00:48:47.150
2/3 of whom are coming
from outside North America.

00:48:47.150 --> 00:48:49.940
No question, courses
like Learning To Program

00:48:49.940 --> 00:48:50.810
are drawing people.

00:48:50.810 --> 00:48:54.140
But people are also drawn to the
incredible breadth and richness

00:48:54.140 --> 00:48:56.720
of what we're pulling from
across the MIT curriculum.

00:48:56.720 --> 00:49:00.020
And this, one of our
most recent publications,

00:49:00.020 --> 00:49:03.270
Black Feminist Health Science
Studies, is an example.

00:49:03.270 --> 00:49:04.610
Next slide, please.

00:49:07.160 --> 00:49:12.550
So OCW at MIT is publishing from
across the entire curriculum,

00:49:12.550 --> 00:49:14.620
all departments, all
programs, ranging

00:49:14.620 --> 00:49:17.950
from introductory undergraduate
to the most advanced graduate

00:49:17.950 --> 00:49:18.560
seminars.

00:49:18.560 --> 00:49:20.630
It's really a tremendous
range of topics.

00:49:20.630 --> 00:49:22.930
So here on the right, a
couple of other examples.

00:49:22.930 --> 00:49:26.680
In addition to learning about
programming, students at MIT

00:49:26.680 --> 00:49:29.440
are learning about the social
and ethical responsibilities

00:49:29.440 --> 00:49:31.390
of computing, and
we're sharing that

00:49:31.390 --> 00:49:34.300
with the world, both for
learners and also resources

00:49:34.300 --> 00:49:36.920
for other teachers to
use in their classroom.

00:49:36.920 --> 00:49:40.210
We're also sharing, for
instance, videos in the moment.

00:49:40.210 --> 00:49:42.670
Down the lower
right, some videos

00:49:42.670 --> 00:49:45.730
that we shared from Professor
Amaido for her seminar

00:49:45.730 --> 00:49:48.710
on reparations for
slavery and colonization,

00:49:48.710 --> 00:49:50.530
which were posted on
our YouTube channel

00:49:50.530 --> 00:49:54.405
so that the public could
engage live in this,

00:49:54.405 --> 00:49:55.780
as it was happening
in the class,

00:49:55.780 --> 00:49:59.050
and those comments could be
fed back into the classroom.

00:49:59.050 --> 00:50:02.050
We have voluntary
participation from our faculty.

00:50:02.050 --> 00:50:06.490
We think about 60%
have participated.

00:50:06.490 --> 00:50:09.550
And we're glad to be able
to have a team of people,

00:50:09.550 --> 00:50:11.980
my colleagues on the
OCW team, who then

00:50:11.980 --> 00:50:14.770
do the work of converting
those classroom materials,

00:50:14.770 --> 00:50:18.440
producing the OER versions
to be shared with the world.

00:50:18.440 --> 00:50:20.320
What's in an MIT OCW course?

00:50:20.320 --> 00:50:23.440
It's really going to vary
from class to class, depending

00:50:23.440 --> 00:50:25.510
on the nature of the subject.

00:50:25.510 --> 00:50:26.410
You might find notes.

00:50:26.410 --> 00:50:29.680
You might find readings,
documentation of activities,

00:50:29.680 --> 00:50:30.760
and assessments.

00:50:30.760 --> 00:50:34.840
You'll also find, increasingly,
contributions from students,

00:50:34.840 --> 00:50:38.980
because their voice really
matters in this experience.

00:50:38.980 --> 00:50:42.520
You'll find more videos
and other rich media.

00:50:42.520 --> 00:50:45.550
And also, interviews
with the MIT faculty

00:50:45.550 --> 00:50:48.340
about how and why they
teach the way they

00:50:48.340 --> 00:50:52.960
do to try to create a dialogue
with other instructors

00:50:52.960 --> 00:50:57.280
around the world who are also
getting into open education.

00:50:57.280 --> 00:50:59.420
Last slide, please.

00:50:59.420 --> 00:51:02.560
So we are excited
later this spring,

00:51:02.560 --> 00:51:06.760
very soon to be launching
next generation OCW.

00:51:06.760 --> 00:51:10.870
There's a new platform, and
it starts with working really

00:51:10.870 --> 00:51:14.290
well on phones, especially
for the many people

00:51:14.290 --> 00:51:16.270
around the world for whom
their phone is either

00:51:16.270 --> 00:51:19.210
their primary or
only digital device.

00:51:19.210 --> 00:51:21.640
Better content discovery
tools, better search

00:51:21.640 --> 00:51:23.140
so that people can
find what they're

00:51:23.140 --> 00:51:25.030
looking for within that
amazing collection.

00:51:25.030 --> 00:51:28.240
But it's not just the
platform and the technology.

00:51:28.240 --> 00:51:29.950
We're also making
new investments

00:51:29.950 --> 00:51:32.320
in enhancing our
programs to ensure

00:51:32.320 --> 00:51:34.360
that the content
that we're bringing

00:51:34.360 --> 00:51:37.510
is constantly vibrant and
current and up to date

00:51:37.510 --> 00:51:42.250
with what's happening at
OCW, and in particular,

00:51:42.250 --> 00:51:45.610
developing and investing
in collaborations

00:51:45.610 --> 00:51:48.370
around the open
education ecosystem.

00:51:48.370 --> 00:51:52.270
In particular, those that are
centering equity and creating

00:51:52.270 --> 00:51:54.220
greater cultural relevance.

00:51:54.220 --> 00:51:58.240
We want to see more culturally
relevant OER all the time,

00:51:58.240 --> 00:51:59.950
and learning how
to work together

00:51:59.950 --> 00:52:02.590
to create and curate
and share this,

00:52:02.590 --> 00:52:04.720
so that we can
collectively continue

00:52:04.720 --> 00:52:08.540
to take on these pressing
global challenges.

00:52:08.540 --> 00:52:10.650
Thank you.

00:52:10.650 --> 00:52:13.810
Now I'd like to kind
of pivot and open it

00:52:13.810 --> 00:52:17.890
up in our last few
minutes here for some Q&amp;A.

00:52:17.890 --> 00:52:23.290
And I'd like to start by
acknowledging just Robbie,

00:52:23.290 --> 00:52:28.420
appreciation for being
so sharp and open hearted

00:52:28.420 --> 00:52:31.390
about the situation
in Ukraine, and using

00:52:31.390 --> 00:52:37.222
our collective capacity
to create this new thing.

00:52:37.222 --> 00:52:38.680
Is there any more
you'd like to say

00:52:38.680 --> 00:52:45.190
about what's led you to open
this Ukraine project up for us?

00:52:45.190 --> 00:52:47.110
Yes, Curt.

00:52:47.110 --> 00:52:50.995
Here, this morning, I'm
getting up with the jitters,

00:52:50.995 --> 00:52:52.750
and ready to present.

00:52:52.750 --> 00:52:54.850
And then I'm
looking at the news,

00:52:54.850 --> 00:53:02.860
and I'm seeing and
feeling what's going on.

00:53:02.860 --> 00:53:04.810
But I'm here drinking coffee.

00:53:04.810 --> 00:53:06.950
And I said, I have
to do something.

00:53:06.950 --> 00:53:09.950
We have to do something.

00:53:09.950 --> 00:53:11.840
And what can we do?

00:53:11.840 --> 00:53:16.030
Number one, we can
capture their culture

00:53:16.030 --> 00:53:19.060
and start a cultural curation.

00:53:19.060 --> 00:53:21.850
Who knows what's going to
happen with their schools,

00:53:21.850 --> 00:53:23.570
with their information.

00:53:23.570 --> 00:53:28.510
But right now, we can grab it,
we can upload it, we can start.

00:53:28.510 --> 00:53:31.990
Number two, it's about
sharing, because they're

00:53:31.990 --> 00:53:33.310
going to need us.

00:53:33.310 --> 00:53:37.600
Just like those over in Haiti
and all other across the world,

00:53:37.600 --> 00:53:40.030
they need open
education resources.

00:53:40.030 --> 00:53:41.770
They don't have books.

00:53:41.770 --> 00:53:46.480
But again, we, our collective,
and I'm so proud of you

00:53:46.480 --> 00:53:47.780
all again.

00:53:47.780 --> 00:53:50.140
I know I was off
script, but I just

00:53:50.140 --> 00:53:53.740
thought that, Curt, there was
a need for us to do something

00:53:53.740 --> 00:53:54.340
about it.

00:53:54.340 --> 00:53:56.420
And I've been watching the chat.

00:53:56.420 --> 00:54:00.040
I've been watching my
team upload those URLs.

00:54:00.040 --> 00:54:03.780
And ladies and gentlemen,
I am proud to let you know,

00:54:03.780 --> 00:54:10.990
we have over 25 entries
for the Ukraine collection.

00:54:10.990 --> 00:54:13.220
This is a blessing.

00:54:13.220 --> 00:54:18.070
But you can see how, with
the HBCUs, we don't compete.

00:54:18.070 --> 00:54:20.320
We complete each other, Curt.

00:54:20.320 --> 00:54:28.240
And we did something amazing
today with this webinar.

00:54:28.240 --> 00:54:29.200
Absolutely.

00:54:29.200 --> 00:54:29.770
Thank you.

00:54:29.770 --> 00:54:32.278
Thank you, Robbie.

00:54:32.278 --> 00:54:34.570
There are a couple of questions
that we got from people

00:54:34.570 --> 00:54:37.540
before the event started.

00:54:37.540 --> 00:54:43.180
Maybe I'll pose these and
see what ideas people have.

00:54:43.180 --> 00:54:48.430
There was a question from Lorita
about any specific information

00:54:48.430 --> 00:54:52.600
about what key coursework
is most important right

00:54:52.600 --> 00:54:56.020
now in the HBCUs?

00:54:56.020 --> 00:54:58.210
What topics or other
characteristics

00:54:58.210 --> 00:55:01.920
are you most focused
on right now?

00:55:01.920 --> 00:55:04.760
And I'm going to let my
colleague Dr. Monique

00:55:04.760 --> 00:55:09.440
Earl-Lewis jump in, because
I think she said it best.

00:55:09.440 --> 00:55:11.390
Thank you, Dr. Melton.

00:55:11.390 --> 00:55:16.520
We are very concerned
about the assault

00:55:16.520 --> 00:55:19.010
on the restrictions
of what individuals

00:55:19.010 --> 00:55:21.170
are able to learn these days.

00:55:21.170 --> 00:55:22.440
We're very concerned.

00:55:22.440 --> 00:55:24.980
We're very concerned about
issues of social justice,

00:55:24.980 --> 00:55:26.840
inclusion, and equity.

00:55:26.840 --> 00:55:29.510
And at Morehouse
College, we are committed

00:55:29.510 --> 00:55:32.420
to making sure that
we are not only

00:55:32.420 --> 00:55:35.600
providing a good
education, but developing

00:55:35.600 --> 00:55:39.620
leaders for the next
generation and engaging them

00:55:39.620 --> 00:55:41.280
in the community.

00:55:41.280 --> 00:55:45.740
So through service learning,
culturally relevant pedagogy,

00:55:45.740 --> 00:55:50.930
and I'm just so pleased with
the ways in which Dr. Melton

00:55:50.930 --> 00:55:53.570
and Gerry Hanley have
advanced this when we first

00:55:53.570 --> 00:55:57.050
became a partner in 2018.

00:55:57.050 --> 00:56:02.480
We have to have courses that
reflect the lived experiences

00:56:02.480 --> 00:56:04.320
of all of our students.

00:56:04.320 --> 00:56:06.650
And it is not just
to benefit students

00:56:06.650 --> 00:56:08.750
of color,
African-American students.

00:56:08.750 --> 00:56:12.230
It's to benefit our white
students, our Asian students,

00:56:12.230 --> 00:56:17.420
our Latinx students, who need to
have an inclusive understanding

00:56:17.420 --> 00:56:20.930
of the history of this
country and of the world,

00:56:20.930 --> 00:56:25.880
because we know identity anchors
our understanding about who

00:56:25.880 --> 00:56:29.220
we are, and how we are
to serve and learn.

00:56:29.220 --> 00:56:31.220
And so we just look
forward to working

00:56:31.220 --> 00:56:34.190
with you and other
members of the community

00:56:34.190 --> 00:56:38.180
through course redesign,
engaging technology, looking

00:56:38.180 --> 00:56:41.000
at different
learning modalities,

00:56:41.000 --> 00:56:43.865
and just bringing our best
game to the table every time.

00:56:47.210 --> 00:56:48.230
Yeah.

00:56:48.230 --> 00:56:51.290
Any other reflections
on that topic?

00:56:51.290 --> 00:57:00.690
Anybody else want to jump in
Thank you, Dr. Earl-Lewis.

00:57:00.690 --> 00:57:04.393
There was a question
we got from Rajeed--

00:57:04.393 --> 00:57:06.060
I think I'm pronouncing
the name right--

00:57:06.060 --> 00:57:09.960
about how we can connect
students in Africa

00:57:09.960 --> 00:57:16.080
with students at HBUs, and
what we could all be learning

00:57:16.080 --> 00:57:18.000
and teaching from each other.

00:57:18.000 --> 00:57:21.390
And maybe start with
Africa, but just

00:57:21.390 --> 00:57:23.520
thinking even more
broadly about how

00:57:23.520 --> 00:57:30.070
OERs spans geographies
and different cultures.

00:57:30.070 --> 00:57:32.100
What experiences
might any of you

00:57:32.100 --> 00:57:35.580
have been having to date
around spanning that sort

00:57:35.580 --> 00:57:38.400
of different communities?

00:57:38.400 --> 00:57:40.560
And again, Curt,
that is the reason

00:57:40.560 --> 00:57:43.260
why we have Dr.
Robert Hassell, who

00:57:43.260 --> 00:57:46.710
is going to jump in, because
we have a student initiative.

00:57:46.710 --> 00:57:51.600
Again, we concentrate,
and we should, on faculty.

00:57:51.600 --> 00:57:56.130
But again, our end result
is to help students.

00:57:56.130 --> 00:58:01.560
So we now have a student
initiative for OER,

00:58:01.560 --> 00:58:03.090
and it is spreading.

00:58:03.090 --> 00:58:08.290
Spreading not only between
the HBCU but across the world.

00:58:08.290 --> 00:58:10.710
So I'm going to let
Dr. Hassell jump in

00:58:10.710 --> 00:58:15.210
and say something about our
student need for open education

00:58:15.210 --> 00:58:15.890
resources.

00:58:19.340 --> 00:58:22.040
I am so excited about
the student education

00:58:22.040 --> 00:58:23.870
and the resources,
because our program

00:58:23.870 --> 00:58:26.720
here and our initiative, our
cultural heritage collections

00:58:26.720 --> 00:58:29.210
program, it helps
our institutions

00:58:29.210 --> 00:58:31.280
meet the complex
challenge of preserving

00:58:31.280 --> 00:58:34.460
large and diverse holdings
of humanities materials

00:58:34.460 --> 00:58:37.430
for future generations,
by supporting them

00:58:37.430 --> 00:58:41.930
in their academic programs
to help them actually access

00:58:41.930 --> 00:58:45.620
the benefit of these
research tools,

00:58:45.620 --> 00:58:49.730
and develop unique strategies
and approaches to assist them

00:58:49.730 --> 00:58:53.690
in constructing their learning
experience as they matriculate.

00:58:53.690 --> 00:58:55.790
It is so important
that we make students

00:58:55.790 --> 00:58:59.180
aware of the endless
possibilities, not only for OER

00:58:59.180 --> 00:59:00.837
to supplement their
learning materials,

00:59:00.837 --> 00:59:03.170
but to enhance their overall
experience in the classroom

00:59:03.170 --> 00:59:05.460
as well.

00:59:05.460 --> 00:59:07.560
Back to you, Dr. Melton.

00:59:07.560 --> 00:59:08.760
And back to you.

00:59:08.760 --> 00:59:12.600
Because I know we're at
the one minute point.

00:59:12.600 --> 00:59:16.810
Let me just say, ladies
and gentlemen, I thank you.

00:59:16.810 --> 00:59:18.330
I thank MIT.

00:59:18.330 --> 00:59:20.190
I thank the Hewlett Foundation.

00:59:20.190 --> 00:59:21.930
And of course, MERLOT.

00:59:21.930 --> 00:59:27.750
But my HBCU sisters
and brothers, again, we

00:59:27.750 --> 00:59:32.230
were able to bring to you
our passion and our purpose.

00:59:32.230 --> 00:59:37.080
And again, yes, it's about
the HBCU for this webinar,

00:59:37.080 --> 00:59:39.690
but we're talking about
underserved population.

00:59:39.690 --> 00:59:44.970
We're talking about access at
any time, any place, anywhere.

00:59:44.970 --> 00:59:47.760
And I want to thank
all of you who helped

00:59:47.760 --> 00:59:50.430
contribute to the Ukraine.

00:59:50.430 --> 00:59:53.610
Because now you can leave
here and say, I did something.

00:59:53.610 --> 00:59:58.470
So again, we thank
you on behalf of HBCU.

00:59:58.470 --> 01:00:03.360
Together we complete,
and we definitely share.

01:00:06.400 --> 01:00:07.150
Yeah.

01:00:07.150 --> 01:00:08.650
Thank you so much, Robbie.

01:00:08.650 --> 01:00:13.210
I can't think of anything
else to say to take us out.

01:00:13.210 --> 01:00:15.040
That was it.

01:00:15.040 --> 01:00:16.900
That was the best.

01:00:16.900 --> 01:00:20.540
Just great, great appreciation
for joining us today.

01:00:20.540 --> 01:00:22.540
And as I said at
the beginning, we

01:00:22.540 --> 01:00:25.660
expect this is the first of
what will be many conversations,

01:00:25.660 --> 01:00:30.820
and stay tuned for more of
these in the months and years

01:00:30.820 --> 01:00:32.560
to come.

01:00:32.560 --> 01:00:33.143
Wish you all--

01:00:33.143 --> 01:00:33.977
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:00:33.977 --> 01:00:34.760
And life is good.

01:00:34.760 --> 01:00:36.990
Life is good.

01:00:36.990 --> 01:00:38.010
Thank you.

01:00:38.010 --> 01:00:39.560
Indeed.

